# ARP Spoofing Detector

Third project in the 100 challenge. Watches ARP traffic on the LAN and raises an alarm when the same IP gets claimed by two different MACs — which is basically what ARP poisoning looks like from the outside.

## What it does

ARP has no authentication. Any device can shout "I'm 192.168.0.1" and everyone believes it, because the protocol was designed in a friendlier era. An attacker doing this gets between you and the gateway — man in the middle. This tool listens for that specific lie: one IP, two claimants.

Prints every ARP claim as it goes past (`who-has` / `is-at` lines), alerts on contradictions, and prints a summary of what it saw when you stop it.

## Requirements

- Python 3, scapy, root (same story as the monitor)

## Usage

```
sudo .venv/bin/python detector.py
```

Leave it running. ctrl+c stops and prints the summary.

## Testing it against myself

A detector that never fires is just a script. So after starting it I sent fake ARP claims from another terminal, pretending two bogus MACs were the gateway:

```
send(ARP(op=2, psrc='192.168.0.1', hwsrc='de:ad:be:ef:ca:fe'))
send(ARP(op=2, psrc='192.168.0.1', hwsrc='de:ad:be:ef:ba:ad'))
```

Both got flagged within the same second:

```
[!!] ARP CONFLICT for 192.168.0.1
     aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff      <- my router's real mac (redacted)
     de:ad:be:ef:ca:fe      <- the fake I sent
     at 10:19:00
```

## Design notes

- Counts both ARP op types. "who-has" and "is-at" both carry a psrc/hwsrc claim, so both are evidence.
- The tricky part was false positives. Legit networks do produce IP-to-new-MAC changes all the time — dhcp renewals, router reboots. So raw "I once saw two MACs for this IP" alarms on normal churn. The fix: only alarm if two *different* claims arrive within a 60 second window. A live attacker has to keep claiming; a router that rebooted two hours ago doesn't.
- That 60s constant is tunable. Thinking about it: a patient attacker could probably space claims out to dodge the window. Known limitation, noted here so I don't forget.
- `filter="arp"` is a BPF filter — the kernel drops non-ARP packets before python even sees them. Much cheaper than checking layers in the callback. Wish I'd known about this for the traffic monitor.

## Weird thing it caught

It flagged a mystery device announcing itself at 192.168.0.241. Spent a while wondering what it was.

Turns out it was my own machine. I'd forgotten it was plugged in over ethernet while also connected on wifi — two interfaces, two IPs, one laptop. So the detector also works as a passive lan mapper, and it apparently knows my setup better than I do.

## Warning

ARP spoofing knocks networks over. Don't send fake claims on any network that isn't yours or a lab — you'll grief everyone's internet, not just your target.

Written as project 3/100.
